# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# STOREFRONT configuration (apps/web). Copy to .env.local and adjust.
# The catalog SYNC JOB is configured separately, in the root .env.example.
# Never paste a real credential into this file: it is tracked by git.
#
# Everything in this file is deployment configuration: a value that genuinely
# differs between a laptop, a preview and production. Brand name, tagline,
# description, currency, contact details, legal identity and feature flags are
# NOT here — they live in `src/config/site.ts`, because there is one shop and
# changing its name or phone number should be a reviewable commit rather than a
# dashboard edit. Do not add them back as variables: `siteConfig` is imported by
# client components, and Next.js does not inline a `NEXT_PUBLIC_*` value that is
# not written as a literal `process.env.NAME` expression.
#
# Copying this whole file into a hosting dashboard is the intended use. Nothing
# here is redundant with a default in code.
#
# `pnpm --filter @catalog/web env:check` reports what a given configuration
# will actually do - which is the question a list of names cannot answer. It
# knows, for instance, that an unset NEXT_PUBLIC_IMAGE_BASE_URL is fine locally
# and fatal on Vercel, and that NEXT_PUBLIC_ENVIRONMENT does nothing there.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# --- Required --------------------------------------------------------------
# The catalog database written by the sync job. Any standard PostgreSQL.
DATABASE_URL=postgres://catalog:catalog@localhost:5433/catalog

# Absolute site URL. Used for canonical tags, Open Graph, the sitemap and
# structured data, so it must be the real public URL in production. The code
# default, when this is unset, is http://localhost:3000 - which is also where
# `pnpm dev` serves.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=http://localhost:3000

# --- Required in production ------------------------------------------------
# Salt for the hashed client fingerprint used by rate limiting. Without a real
# random value the fingerprint is predictable across deployments. The raw IP
# address is never stored.
#
# Rate limiting keeps working without it, which is exactly the danger: nothing
# breaks, so nothing tells you. A production process now logs an error at
# startup, and `env:check --strict` refuses. `openssl rand -hex 32`.
RATE_LIMIT_SALT=

# --- Images ----------------------------------------------------------------
# Public base URL of the object storage or CDN holding mirrored product images.
# Leave empty in development: images are then served from the local storage
# directory through the app's own /media route.
#
# REQUIRED on any host without a persistent disk, Vercel included. The /media
# fallback reads STORAGE_LOCAL_DIR off local disk - a directory the scraper
# wrote on someone's laptop, which is not in the deployment - so leaving this
# empty there means every product image 404s.
#
# It is also read at BUILD time by next.config.ts to build
# `images.remotePatterns`, the enforcement point for never hotlinking the
# source domain. So changing it needs a redeploy, not just a dashboard save -
# and a value that fails to parse as a URL silently allows no remote images at
# all.
NEXT_PUBLIC_IMAGE_BASE_URL=
# Where the sync wrote images when STORAGE_DRIVER=local. Relative paths are
# resolved from the app directory.
STORAGE_LOCAL_DIR=../../.storage

# --- Optional --------------------------------------------------------------
# Connection pool size, per process. Defaults to 10 on a long-running server
# and 2 on Vercel, where every concurrent function instance is its own process
# with its own pool and 10 quietly becomes 10 x N. Raise it only when pointing
# at a pooler that can take more.
# DATABASE_POOL_MAX=10

# Which environment this is, for robots.txt. Only needed on a host that does not
# announce its own environment. On Vercel, `VERCEL_ENV` already does, and it
# takes precedence — leave this unset there. Anything other than "production"
# makes robots.txt disallow everything, which is what keeps a staging
# deployment out of search results.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_ENVIRONMENT=production
